WEBVTT

00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:07.462
The fourth conversation is about decisions: how do we make them?

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:12.382
In many teams, the problem isn’t bad decisions. It’s unclear ones.

00:00:12.432 --> 00:00:18.302
Nobody is quite sure who decides, so decisions get made in hallways, or not at all.

00:00:18.352 --> 00:00:23.362
Or they get made, and then reopened at the next meeting, because someone wasn’t asked.

00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:28.502
Here are the signs. The same topic shows up on the agenda again and again.

00:00:28.552 --> 00:00:32.682
People ask “who decided this?” more often than “why?”.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:36.122
Everything goes up to the manager, even small things.

00:00:36.172 --> 00:00:40.422
Or meetings end with a good discussion, and no clear decision.

00:00:40.472 --> 00:00:51.062
The output on the canvas is a short list: the decisions that come up often, who decides each one, who should be asked, who should be told, and how the decision gets made.

00:00:51.112 --> 00:00:53.502
That “how” is the interesting part.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:57.622
There are many ways to decide, and each one fits a different situation.

00:00:57.672 --> 00:01:00.962
On the canvas you’ll find twelve, in four groups.

00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:04.322
Some help you narrow down options, like dot voting.

00:01:04.372 --> 00:01:09.322
Some help you decide quickly, like majority vote or one person with authority.

00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:13.342
Some bring everyone along, like consent or fist to five.

00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:17.370
And some settle who has the final say, like delegation.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.550
No method is best. Consensus is great when everyone must carry the decision, but it’s slow.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.710
Authority is fast, but people may not commit.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:32.911
The skill is to choose the method on purpose, before the discussion starts, instead of discovering it at the end.

